('69771fb0-d45a-4f89-814a-67a3f320ca16', 83, 'Gazing into Eternity', 3, 'So we fix our eyes not on what is seen, but on what is unseen, since what is seen is temporary, but what is unseen is eternal.', '2 Corinthians 4:18', 'In every season of life, God\'s Word arrives not merely as advice, but as a living anchor for the human soul. Today, in the context of unshakable faith & radical trust, the truth of 2 Corinthians 4:18 invites you to shift your focus away from momentary anxieties and onto the enduring character of God.\n\nConsider what is currently contending for your peace. Our natural inclination is often to strive in our own strength, to manage every outcome, or to replay yesterday\'s missteps. Yet scripture declares: "So we fix our eyes not on what is seen, but on what is unseen, since what is seen is temporary, but what is unseen is eternal." When you absorb this promise, your perspective transforms from human scarcity to heavenly abundance. God is already present in your tomorrow, orchestrating every circumstance with unfailing wisdom and grace.\n\n**Today\'s Interactive Practice:** Pause right now for sixty seconds. Inhale deeply through your nose, acknowledging God\'s breath in your lungs. As you slowly exhale, surrender the one situation you have been trying to control today. Whisper quietly: \'Lord, I trust Your heart, I stand on 2 Corinthians 4:18, and I walk in Your peace.\'', 'Heavenly Father, I come before You today with an open heart, grateful for the living power of Your Word in 2 Corinthians 4:18. Forgive me for the moments I allow worry or self-reliance to cloud my vision of Your goodness. Holy Spirit, anchor my thoughts in the truth that so we fix our eyes not on what is seen, but on what is unseen, since what is seen is temporary, but what is unseen is eternal. Grant me discernment to recognize Your guidance, courage to walk by faith, and a spirit overflowed with gratitude. I dedicate my words, my work, and my relationships today to Your glory